---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "Deck Stain Drying Time", url: "/deck-stain-drying-time/" },
    { title: "Deck Stain Not Drying", url: "/deck-stain-not-drying/" },
    { title: "What Temperature to Stain a Deck", url: "/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-09", modified: "2026-09-09" });
const title = "Rain After Staining a Deck: Is It Ruined?";
const description =
  "What rain does to fresh deck stain at one hour, six hours and a day, how to judge the damage once it dries, and the repairs that actually work.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Guides", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Rain After Staining a Deck", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/rain-after-staining-a-deck/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is my deck ruined if it rained after staining?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Almost certainly not. Most rain damage on fresh stain is cosmetic — water spotting, uneven sheen and light patches — and a surprising amount of it fades once the wood dries fully and sees a few days of sun. Genuine ruin means stain physically washed off the boards, which requires heavy rain within the first hour or two. Do nothing until the deck is completely dry, then judge it in good light." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long after staining a deck can it rain?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Plan on 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Some water-based products claim rain resistance in four to six hours, and that holds only in warm, low-humidity conditions with a thin coat. Oil-based stains need longer, typically a full 24 hours before rain will not mark them. Rain inside those windows does not necessarily ruin anything, but it will leave marks." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What should you do immediately if it rains on fresh stain?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Nothing at all, and that is the hard part. Do not wipe, blot, squeegee or blow water off an uncured finish — every one of those drags the stain and turns a temporary water mark into a permanent smear. Let the deck dry completely, which may take two or three days after a soaking, then assess it dry and in daylight." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will water spots on deck stain go away?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Often, yes. Spotting caused by rain between roughly four and twenty-four hours after application is usually a difference in sheen rather than in pigment, and several days of sun and normal weathering blends much of it out. Give it two weeks before deciding it is permanent — a great many decks that looked ruined on the Monday look fine by the following weekend." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do you fix rain damage on a stained deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "It depends on the damage. Light spotting usually needs nothing. Uneven sheen across a whole board is best fixed by a light maintenance coat over that area once the deck is fully cured. Washed-out patches where bare wood shows need spot cleaning, light sanding to feather the edges, and restaining that area. Only widespread lifting requires stripping and starting again." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you restain a deck after rain damage?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Yes, but wait for the wood to reach its normal moisture content first — two to four days of dry weather after the rain, longer in humidity or shade. Applying more stain over a deck that is still holding water traps the moisture and produces a tacky finish that never cures properly, which is a worse problem than the spotting you were fixing." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does rain affect oil-based and water-based stain differently?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Yes. Water-based stains form a film as the water evaporates and are more vulnerable early but more resistant sooner, often shrugging off rain after four to six hours in good conditions. Oil-based stains cure by oxidation, which is slower, so they stay vulnerable for longer — typically the full 24 hours — but they tend to spot rather than wash off, because the oil has already penetrated." },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What about dew instead of rain?",
          acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: "Dew catches out the people who watched the forecast most carefully. On a clear, calm night the deck surface radiates heat away and drops below the dew point, so moisture condenses directly onto a curing finish even though no rain fell. If overnight lows are within a few degrees of the dew point, stain in the morning rather than the afternoon so the coat has a full day of sun before nightfall." },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Stone & Wood Sealers",
    tags: ["rain after staining a deck", "deck stain rain", "deck stain troubleshooting", "deck staining"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Deck Stains", link: "/best-deck-stain-reviews/" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">Troubleshooting</span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">Field Tested</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">It <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Rained</span> After I Stained My Deck</h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Probably not ruined — and the single most damaging thing you can do right now is go outside and try to fix it.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#do-nothing" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">What to Do Right Now</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">Jump to the FAQ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto"><SocialProof users="9.2k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#do-nothing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Do Nothing First</a>
          <a href="#timing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What the Timing Tells You</a>
          <a href="#assess" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Assessing the Damage</a>
          <a href="#fixes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Fix for Each Case</a>
          <a href="#oil-vs-water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Oil vs Water-Based</a>
          <a href="#dew" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Dew Problem</a>
          <a href="#prevention" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Not Repeating It</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          You checked the forecast, it said clear, and at four in the afternoon the sky opened. The deck now looks mottled and awful. Before anything else: most of what you are looking at is wet wood, and wet wood is not a diagnosis.
        </p>

        <div id="do-nothing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Do Nothing. Genuinely.</h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF4500] mb-3">The One Rule</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Do not wipe, blot, squeegee, towel or blow water off an uncured finish.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A stain that has not set is still mobile. Anything that touches it drags pigment sideways and turns a water mark — which may well fade on its own — into a smear that will not. The urge to go out there with a towel is strong and it is the single most common way people convert a recoverable deck into a genuine repair job.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The second thing not to do is judge the result while the deck is wet. Wet wood is dark, blotchy and uneven regardless of what the finish is doing, and a deck that looks catastrophic under standing water frequently looks unremarkable three days later. Photographs taken in the rain have talked more people into unnecessary restaining than any other single factor.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you should do is limited and dull. Move furniture off if it is still off. If there are puddles sitting in low spots and the rain has stopped, you can gently sweep water toward the gaps between boards using the flat of a broom held just above the surface without contacting the finish — but if in doubt, leave it. Then wait.
          </p>

          <p>
            Waiting means the full drying period plus a bit: two to three days of dry weather before you assess, and longer if it stays humid or the deck is shaded. There is nothing to be gained by looking at it sooner, and quite a lot to lose by acting on what you see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What the Timing Tells You</h2>

          <p>
            How long the stain had before the rain arrived predicts the outcome better than how hard it rained.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rain Arrived</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Likely Result</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Outlook</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 1 hour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stain diluted and physically washed off in runs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Real repair needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1-4 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Light patches, streaking on slopes and edges</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spot repair likely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4-12 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water spotting, uneven sheen, mottling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually cosmetic, often self-correcting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">12-24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faint spotting, slight sheen difference</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Almost always fades out</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over 24-48 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing — this is what the finish is for</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No action</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two variables shift those rows. Warm, breezy, low-humidity conditions move everything up a band, because the stain sets faster — a coat applied at 80°F with a breeze may be effectively rain-safe in half the time of the same coat applied at 60°F in still, damp air. And a thin, properly wiped-back coat resists rain far better than a heavy one that is still sitting wet on the surface, which is one more reason not to over-apply.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing worth knowing is that the deck's own drainage decides where damage concentrates. Boards that shed water do better than boards where it pools, and the low corner of a slightly out-of-level deck often carries most of the marking on an otherwise fine job. Our <a href="/deck-stain-drying-time/">deck stain drying time guide</a> covers the four separate stages a coat passes through and where rain resistance sits among them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="assess" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Assessing the Damage Once It Is Dry</h2>

          <p>
            Wait for two or three consecutive dry days, then walk the deck in good daylight — mid-morning or late afternoon, when raking light shows sheen differences that midday sun flattens out. Look for four distinct things, because they need four different responses.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Spotting</h3>
          <p>
            Discrete round or teardrop marks, slightly different in sheen from the surrounding board but the same colour. This is the most common outcome and the least serious — the pigment is where it should be and only the surface finish varies. In most cases sun and a few weeks of normal weathering blend it out entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Uneven Sheen Across Whole Boards</h3>
          <p>
            One board or one area reads glossier or flatter than its neighbours. The colour is right; the surface is not. This usually resolves partially over a season and can be evened out deliberately with a light maintenance coat once the deck has fully cured.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Light Patches Where Colour Is Genuinely Thin</h3>
          <p>
            Areas that are visibly paler than the rest, usually on the slopes, edges and end grain where water ran rather than sat. Here the stain really did wash away before it set, and the fix is to put more stain back — but only on those areas.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bare Wood or Lifted Film</h3>
          <p>
            Wood showing through, or, with a solid stain, a film that has lifted at edges. This is the only outcome that counts as a genuine failure, and it takes heavy rain inside the first hour or two to produce it across any meaningful area.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Give It Two Weeks Before You Call It Permanent</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A great many decks that look badly marked three days after the rain look acceptable a fortnight later, once UV and normal weathering have evened the surface out. Restaining a deck that would have self-corrected costs a weekend and a hundred dollars, and it also risks over-applying stain to a surface that is already saturated — which causes the tacky, never-drying finish covered in <a href="/deck-stain-not-drying/" class="text-amber-900 underline">deck stain not drying</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Fix for Each Case</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>For water spotting:</strong> nothing. Wait a fortnight, look again, and in most cases it will have gone or faded far enough not to matter from standing height. If it genuinely persists on an otherwise sound coat, a very light maintenance coat over the affected area evens the sheen.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For uneven sheen:</strong> let the deck cure fully — one to three days for touch, but a full week or two before recoating anything — then apply a thin maintenance coat across the whole affected board or section rather than spot-dabbing. Work board by board along the full length so there is no lap mark mid-board, and wipe back anything not absorbed within fifteen minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For light or washed-out patches:</strong> clean the area with a mild deck cleaner and rinse, let it dry fully, then restain that section, feathering out into the sound surrounding stain. Match the batch if you can — leftover stain from the same can is ideal, which is a good argument for never throwing the last inch away. Some colour difference is likely at first and normally closes within a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>For bare wood or lifted film:</strong> this is the case that needs real work. Sand the affected area to sound material and feather the edges, clean and brighten it, let it dry, then restain. If lifting is widespread across a solid-stained deck, stripping and starting over is the honest answer, and <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">how to strip deck stain</a> covers it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Across all four, resist the temptation to apply a full second coat over the whole deck as a corrective measure. A penetrating stain only holds what the wood can absorb, and a deck that already has a coat in it will leave the surplus sitting on the surface — where it stays tacky, collects dirt and eventually peels. More stain is very rarely the answer to a stain problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oil-vs-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Oil-Based and Water-Based Behave Differently</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Water-based stains</strong> set as the water evaporates, which is relatively fast in warm, dry, moving air. They are highly vulnerable for the first hour or two and then become resistant comparatively quickly — many are genuinely rain-safe at four to six hours in good conditions. When they do fail, they tend to fail by washing off, because the binder had not yet formed a continuous film.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Oil-based stains</strong> cure by oxidation, a chemical process that does not care much how breezy it is and cannot be rushed. They stay vulnerable longer — plan on a full 24 hours — but their failure mode is gentler. The oil has already penetrated into the wood, so rain typically leaves surface spotting and sheen variation rather than stripping pigment away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practically, that means an oil-based deck caught by rain at six hours is usually in better shape than a water-based deck caught at ninety minutes, even though the water-based product has the shorter published rain window. The comparison in full is in <a href="/oil-based-vs-water-based-deck-stain/">oil-based versus water-based deck stain</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solid stains sit somewhere else again. They form a genuine film, so early rain can leave craters and pinholes in that film rather than dilute marks, and those do not weather out — they need the affected area sanded flat and recoated. The upside is that a solid stain's opacity hides a repair far more completely than a semi-transparent one does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dew" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When It Was Not Rain At All</h2>

          <p>
            A significant share of the people who arrive at this problem never had rain. They stained on a clear afternoon, the forecast stayed dry all night, and they woke up to a spotted deck anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            The culprit is dew. On a clear, calm night a horizontal surface radiates heat to the sky and drops several degrees below the air temperature — often below the dew point — and moisture condenses directly onto the curing finish. The deck was rained on by its own microclimate, and no forecast would have warned you.
          </p>

          <p>
            The defence is timing rather than luck. Stain in the morning, not the afternoon, so the coat gets a full day of sun and warmth before nightfall. Check the overnight low against the dew point rather than just the rain probability; if they are within a few degrees, condensation is likely. And be more cautious in autumn, when clear nights and big day-night temperature swings make dew far heavier than in midsummer. Our guide to <a href="/what-temperature-to-stain-a-deck/">what temperature to stain a deck</a> covers the numbers that matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Not Repeating It Next Time</h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Read a full 48-hour forecast, not the afternoon.</strong> A clear day followed by an overnight front is the classic trap.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Start in the morning.</strong> Every hour of daylight after the last coat is protection you cannot buy any other way.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Apply thin and wipe back the surplus.</strong> A thin coat sets faster and resists rain sooner. A heavy one stays vulnerable for hours longer.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Work in sections you can finish.</strong> If weather threatens, a completed section that has had two hours beats a whole deck that has had ten minutes.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Check the dew point, not only the rain chance.</strong> Overnight lows near the dew point mean condensation regardless of the forecast.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Keep the leftover stain.</strong> Sealed and labelled, it is the batch-matched repair kit for exactly this situation.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Wiping the Water Off</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An uncured stain is still mobile, and a towel drags pigment sideways. This converts a water mark that would probably have faded into a permanent smear. Whatever the instinct says, leave it alone until the deck is completely dry.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging It Wet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Wet wood is dark and blotchy no matter what the finish is doing. Decks that look destroyed in the rain routinely look fine three days later. Assess only after two or three consecutive dry days, in raking daylight.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Restaining the Whole Deck Immediately</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The wood is still holding rainwater and already carries a coat. More stain on top has nowhere to go, so it sits on the surface, stays tacky and eventually peels. Wait, assess, and repair only what genuinely needs it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spot-Dabbing Repairs</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Dabbing stain onto individual marks leaves a leopard pattern that is more obvious than the spotting was. Repair by the board, along its full length, feathering into the sound finish at each end.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Same-Day Forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most stains need 24 to 48 hours of dry weather, so an afternoon that looks clear is not enough information. Check the full two-day picture, including the overnight dew point, before opening the can.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is my deck ruined if it rained after staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Almost certainly not. Most rain damage is cosmetic — spotting, uneven sheen, light patches — and much of it fades once the wood dries and sees sun. Genuine ruin means stain physically washed off, which needs heavy rain inside the first hour or two. Do nothing until it is fully dry.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long after staining can it rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Plan on 24 to 48 hours of dry weather. Some water-based products claim rain resistance at four to six hours, which holds only in warm, dry conditions with a thin coat. Oil-based stains generally need the full 24 hours before rain stops marking them.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should you do immediately?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Nothing — and that is the hard part. Do not wipe, blot, squeegee or blow water off an uncured finish; all of those drag stain and make a temporary mark permanent. Let it dry completely, which may take two or three days, then assess in daylight.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will water spots go away?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Often. Spotting from rain between four and twenty-four hours after application is usually a sheen difference rather than a pigment one, and sun plus normal weathering blends much of it out. Give it two weeks before deciding it is permanent.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you fix rain damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">By severity. Light spotting needs nothing. Uneven sheen takes a light maintenance coat over that area once cured. Washed-out patches need cleaning, light sanding to feather edges, and restaining that area. Only widespread lifting justifies stripping and starting again.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you restain after rain damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes, once the wood is back to normal moisture — two to four dry days, longer in humidity or shade. Staining over a deck still holding water traps the moisture and produces a tacky finish that never cures, which is worse than the spotting you were fixing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do oil and water-based stains react differently?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes. Water-based sets as water evaporates — vulnerable early, resistant sooner, and it fails by washing off. Oil-based cures by oxidation, so it stays vulnerable longer but has already penetrated, meaning it tends to spot rather than strip away.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if it was dew rather than rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Dew catches the people who checked the forecast most carefully. On a clear, calm night the deck drops below the dew point and moisture condenses on the curing finish. If overnight lows sit near the dew point, stain in the morning so the coat gets a full day of sun.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

