        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Do nothing right now.</strong> Wiping, blotting or squeegeeing an uncured stain drags pigment and makes a temporary mark permanent. Let the deck dry for two or three days, then assess it dry, in raking daylight.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Timing predicts the outcome. Rain inside the first hour genuinely washes stain off and needs repair; rain after four to twelve hours usually leaves spotting and sheen differences that weather out; rain after 24 hours generally does nothing at all. Give any marking a fortnight before calling it permanent.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When repair is needed, repair only what needs it — by the board, along its full length, feathered into sound finish. Never respond by flooding the whole deck with another coat: the wood cannot absorb it, and the surplus goes tacky and peels.
          </p>
